d_pruning_c910b8a1_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In branch-and-bound for a maximization integer program, a node's LP relaxation optimum is integral with value larger than the incumbent value $L$. What is done with this node?","options":["It becomes the incumbent, and the node is pruned.","It is pruned, and the incumbent value $L$ is kept.","It becomes the incumbent, and the node is branched.","It is branched, since only leaf nodes are pruned."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1f2ae1c4b503d8485cb939e0","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_branch_and_bound_pruning_c910b8a1_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For: maximize $3x_1+2x_2$ subject to $2x_1+2x_2\\le9.5$, $x_1\\le3.5$, $x_1,x_2\\ge0$ integer, branch-and-bound reaches the node with branch constraints $x_2\\ge3$ and $x_1\\ge2$. What happens to this node?","options":["It is pruned: its bound is below the incumbent.","It gets the integral optimum $(2,3)$, value 12.","It is pruned as infeasible: $2\\cdot2+2\\cdot3=10>9.5$.","It is branched on $x_1$, as $x_1\\le3.5$ allows more."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"92e092bafea9fe934d70c144","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}